{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>But if the scab spreads, etc.</b> This is a sign that nature does not have the power to cast off from him all the impurity, for it is abundant. And this too is a proof that he has not yet cleansed himself from his sin — his impurity is still upon him. But if the plague has not spread, nature has already cast off from him every eruption and impurity — this is a proof that he has already resolved in his heart to return in complete repentance. And if the tzara'at has covered all the skin of the plague from his head to his feet, nature has already cast off all the illness to the outside, and then without doubt he will be healed; therefore he is pure. And likewise from the aspect of punishment, without doubt — if he sees that the tzara'at has spread over his entire body, then all the more will he resolve in his heart to return in repentance, unless there be in him healthy flesh, for this is a proof that through all of this he will not be chastised. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, likened the matter to water: all broad waters are not deep, and the deep ones are not broad. So too, if the plague is not broad, then it penetrates deep into the body, and there is reason to fear the strengthening of the illness; but if it is broad and spreads over all the skin, it is not deep in the body and will heal quickly." "And our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 97a), learned from this a way of derash: the Mashiach does not come until the entire kingdom has turned to heresy, as it says: ״כֻּלּוֹ הָפַךְ לָבָן טָהוֹר הוּא״ — a hint to the matter: ״הָפַךְ לָבָן״, when the word is reversed, becomes ״נָבָל״, and their intention is to say: when they all blaspheme (ינבלו) the Rock of their salvation, then HaShem will of necessity cleanse all the earth of its idols. And their proof is from this plague which comes by way of punishment — and when it has all turned white, the sinner will surely take it to heart to return; so too Israel, when they see that the entire kingdom has turned to heresy and they see that there is none shut up and none forsaken, surely they will then take it to heart to return in complete repentance to HaShem — and then ״טָהוֹר הוּא״, he is pure." "And the matter of ״quick raw flesh in the swelling״ — on the surface the flesh appears healthy, and beneath it stands the bright spot. Or perhaps, by way of punishment, this plague comes upon the class of hypocrites who are dyed, who show themselves as fit on the outside while their inside is full of deceit and rottenness — therefore this kind comes upon them, measure for measure."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {1 "The wordplay הָפַךְ לָבָן → נָבָל (reversing the letters of לָבָן) is rendered literally; the verb ינבלו derives from the root נ-ב-ל in the sense of blaspheme/wither, linking to נבל as a flippant blasphemous act (Psalms 74:18).", 2 "תֹךְ in the phrase מִרְמוֹת וָתֹךְ rendered as 'rottenness' following the root תּוֹךְ/תִּיכוֹךְ (corruption), paralleling מִרְמוֹת; alternately it may carry the literal sense 'that which is within,' reinforcing the inside/outside contrast."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:26.674524161Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}